ipcd: a capture restart no longer hangs

New stream settings from the web page, an IP address lost for more than 20
seconds and new Wi-Fi settings while online all stop and start the capture. The
stop waited for the watchdog thread, which since 63e72fb runs for as long as the
process does, so it never came back. The stream stayed away until someone pulled
the plug, and as the thread went on feeding, the watchdog did not help either.

Nobody waits for that thread now, and the next start leaves it running. With the
restart working, the paths around it matter too:

- The stop waits 60 seconds at most. Then the feeding ends and the watchdog restarts
  the camera, so that a stop hanging in an SDK call cannot have the same result again.
- A restart from the web page is one step for the main loop, which could otherwise
  see the capture stopped and start a second one. It takes the settings when its turn
  comes, so that the last of two quick changes wins, and changes saved while it waits
  need no restart of their own. Saving no longer trips over a second save. cap_run is
  set before the supervisor starts, as a stop right after a start would otherwise get
  lost.
- After an error in the supervisor, capture_is_running() says so, and the main loop
  really starts a new capture. capture_start() used to return at once, as the old
  one still counted as started. If no stream could start, the supervisor gives up
  as well, so that the main loop tries again. That heals a passing error. A lasting
  one still means no picture and a fed watchdog, with a new try every few seconds.
- RTSP clients are disconnected at once. Nothing woke their threads, so they outlived
  the restart and kept the connection open without ever sending a picture again. A
  thread stuck in a send can sleep through the whole restart, so besides the flag,
  which the next start clears, it now looks at a counter.
- The web page refuses an RTSP port that something else listens on. The capture could
  not start on it, again and again.
- rtsp_server_shutdown() no longer joins an accept thread that never started, as
  after a failed bind.
- The pools take the whole DMA reservation, and after a stop the kernel can still be
  clearing the old ones, at idle priority. So activate is tried for up to 5 seconds
  before the start counts as failed.
- udhcpc gets -b as everywhere else. Without a lease it never returned, so after a
  lost address the main loop stood still while the watchdog was being fed.
- Starting the capture loads the sensor configuration again, which undoes the writes
  in /etc/config/sensor.init. They now follow at once, as the loop in S60ipcd can
  take a minute, and until then the picture can be upside down.
- With IR detection on, a day or night mode forced by hand stays over a restart.
- The snapshot code uses the encoder number the SDK gave it. With the main stream off
  the number 2 was wrong, the channel stayed open and in the way of the next start.
- The frame rate is limited to 30. The web page offered 60, but from about 35 no stream
  delivers a picture, on the GT1 Pro and on the GC3. It refuses a higher value now, and
  one in a saved configuration counts as 30.

